Desperation is not a standard economic construct like risk aversion or time preference
Most existing literature ties in to criminal behavior when discussing desperation

Initial concept and interest for project comes from idea of political game:

Game: Two parties are engaged in conflict (this can be a purely economic conflict or in the form of "warfare") in which one party is significantly "larger" than the other party. 
This "larger" can mean any type of overwhelming advantage over the other party.
Let's assume that this conflict presents an existential threat to the smaller party, while the larger party has no such threat of independence.
The object of the game is to get the other side to conceide. There is no goal to achieve outside of the other party stepping down.
As part of the game, there is a monetary component involving a system of credit. The smaller party obviously will have a smaller pool to work with, but the nature of the conflict will cause them to behave more irratic. 
Because of the existencial threat, the smaller party will take as many loans as are made available to them. If they lose the conflict then they don't have to worry about paying back the credit. If they win, they live.
Living with an exorbitant amount of bad credit is better than ceasing to exist, so naturally there is no incentive to concede for the smaller party.

The larger party has no possibility of ceasing to exist from the game itself. Any possibility comes purely from outside factors. The larger party has more access to resources but still has access to the same credit system 
that the smaller party has. The larger party will not have the same incentive to be burried in credit as their back is not up against a wall. While the proportional cost of the game might weigh more against the smaller 
party (as they will likely spend more than 100% of the resources that they have available), the larger party will likely potentially face a higher up front real cost from the game. 

Even if the larger party has a large stake in winning the game, their tolerance to drop out will likely be higher than that of the smaller party due to the element of desperation. 

Is it possible to model this tolerance? If so, and we can integrate dollar amounts to both sides, we should be able to weigh real world conflicts to determine the likelyhood of a side "dropping out of or winning the game".
